      "sequence_position": 15,
      "problem": "If $Y=\\{1\\}$ and $Z=\\{m,n\\}$ then $|P(Z)\\times P(Y\\times Z)|=$",
      "worked_solution": "For any set $S,$ we have $|P(S)|={2}^{|S|}.$ Also, $|S\\times T|=|S|\\cdot |T|.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n|Y|=1,\\,|Z|=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(Y\\times Z)| & ={2}^{|Y\\times Z|} \\\\  & ={2}^{|Y|\\,\\cdot \\,|Z|} \\\\  & ={2}^{1\\cdot 2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(Z)\\times P(Y\\times Z)| & =|P(Z)|\\cdot |P(Y\\times Z)| \\\\  & ={2}^{|Z|}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
